# Origin:  clearcotelabs/clearcote-browser -- rewritten against the same call sites, not copied.
# Source:  patches/140-screen.patch and patches/141-media-queries.patch
# Commit:  clearcote-browser-main tarball, audited 2026-08-24 (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.5)
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: substantial -- this is a rewrite, and the header says so rather than claiming a port.
#          Both counterparts read an `ungoogled::CurrentPersona()` struct Veil deliberately does not
#          carry (6.9: *one identity, one source, and the source is the one the UI can name*). `140`
#          holds 56 references to it and `141` holds 27, so there is no ladder left to copy once the
#          middle rung goes. What is taken from clearcote is the *set of call sites* -- which getters
#          have to move together for a display to be coherent -- and that is the expensive half.
#
#          Point by point, against the two source patches:
#          - the persona rung is deleted everywhere, leaving `switch > host`. clearcote's own
#            `ScreenIntOverride()` already had that shape (6.9), which is why this is cheap.
#          - `--fingerprint-device-pixel-ratio` becomes `--fingerprint-device-scale-factor`: the name
#            Veil's `000` already declares, and Chromium's own name for the quantity
#            (`display::Display::device_scale_factor()`). Settled in 6.9, not here.
#          - `--fingerprint-avail-width`, `--fingerprint-avail-height` and `--fingerprint-color-depth`
#            are **not** taken. The work area is the panel minus the claimed shell's one bounded
#            strip, and the colour depth is 24 on every desktop a persona would claim, so both are
#            derived here. A switch for the work area is a way to fail assertion 6 R1 by hand (6.9).
#          - `141`'s pointer / hover / dynamic-range / colour-gamut hunks and its
#            `media_values_cached.h` default flip are left out entirely. They describe an input class
#            and a colour space, not a display panel; `(hover:)`, `(any-pointer:)` and
#            `(dynamic-range:)` are in Veil's baseline and must not move here. The mobile input story
#            is T13's and T24's.
#          - `140`'s `outerWidth`/`outerHeight` clamping is left out and its `screenX`/`screenY`
#            offset fabrication is replaced. See "The window is real" below.
#          - `ScreenDetailed::isInternal()` is left alone. clearcote answers it from
#            `persona.mobile`; Veil models no laptop/desktop distinction, so there is nothing to
#            derive it from and a guess would be a value that agrees with nothing.
#
# Veil-authored `9NN` numbering: `960` has no `060` to correct, so it is in the `950`-`999` range
# reserved for patches with no direct counterpart -- which is what a rewrite is.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The display block: eight surfaces, three switches, one derivation.
#
# `000` has declared `--fingerprint-screen-width`, `--fingerprint-screen-height` and
# `--fingerprint-device-scale-factor` since it was ported, propagated all three into every renderer,
# and **nothing has ever read one**. This is the patch that reads them. Until it landed, a profile
# could be reported as presenting a 1470x956 @2x panel while `screen.width` was still 2560 -- the
# same defect as emitting a switch no Chromium has, and harder to see (6.9).
#
# Taken (the user's claim about the machine, derivable from nothing):
#
#   --fingerprint-screen-width=<int>          screen.width, (device-width:)
#   --fingerprint-screen-height=<int>         screen.height, (device-height:)
#   --fingerprint-device-scale-factor=<num>   window.devicePixelRatio, (resolution:),
#                                             ScreenDetailed.devicePixelRatio
#
# Derived from the panel and from `--fingerprint-platform` (no switch, one source):
#
#   screen.availWidth   = panel width
#   screen.availHeight  = panel height minus the claimed shell's reserved strip
#   screen.availLeft    = 0
#   screen.availTop     = the top half of that strip
#   screen.colorDepth   = 24, and screen.pixelDepth is Blink's alias of it
#   screen.isExtended   = false -- a persona is one display, and `true` leaks a real second monitor
#   screen.orientation  = landscape-primary / portrait-primary from the panel's own aspect, angle 0
#   (color: n)          = 24 / 3, so the media-query side cannot disagree with screen.colorDepth
#
# The reserved strip is one number per claimed shell, in `display_persona.h`. It is a statement
# about where an operating system puts its shell, which is the only kind of claim assertion 6
# permits -- never a hardware spec. Windows reserves 48 px at the bottom (measured: this host's own
# 1440-px panel reports availHeight 1392), macOS 25 px at the top for the menu bar, GNOME 27 px at
# the top. macOS is modelled with the Dock auto-hidden, because a Dock height depends on user
# settings Veil does not model while the menu bar does not.
#
# **The window is real, and that is the divergence from `140` worth reading twice.** clearcote clamps
# `outerHeight` to the persona's work area because its window may be larger than the panel it claims.
# Veil forbids that configuration at save time instead -- `veil_core::validate` rejects a window that
# does not fit the derived work area -- so `outerWidth`/`outerHeight` stay a true measurement of the
# window that actually exists. What Veil cannot forbid is where the window manager *put* it: a real
# x of 900 measured against a declared 1470-px panel puts the window off an edge the page believes
# in. So `screenX`/`screenY` are clamped into the work area and otherwise returned unchanged, which
# means this hunk does nothing at all on a host that placed the window inside the declared area.
# `140`'s `16 + seed % 160` offset is not ported: a fabricated position is a second source for a fact
# the real window already answers.
#
# Nothing here touches `display::ScreenInfo`, the compositor or layout. `--force-device-scale-factor`
# is stock Chromium, works, and is *not* a cheaper version of this patch: it moves the real scale
# factor, so the viewport and the window move with it while `screen.width` stays the host's (6.9).
# `960` moves what JS reports and leaves the real surface alone, which is why the `default` case --
# emitting none of the three switches -- must be byte-identical to the pre-`960` capture.

